## Summary

This PR changes the milestone direction from benchmark/research expansion to **core v1 completeness**. It makes `sqrt` a standalone C++23 square-root algorithm, correctness, and theory repository that can later shed its empirical benchmark layer into `EddyTodd/bench` without modifying permanent algorithm implementations.

The repository was audited from the actual `main` implementation first. The main core gaps found were:

- `sqrtlab` directly linked `experiment.cpp` and `float_experiment.cpp`, coupling the reusable library to timing/statistics infrastructure;
- benchmark/research executables were unconditionally part of the normal build;
- no install/export/package configuration existed;
- architecture-specific scalar functions could silently call `std::sqrt` on unsupported targets even though capability queries said the method was unsupported;
- binary64 Remez existed as seed research but was not part of the stable method registry;
- native binary32 lacked several genuinely distinct mechanism families represented in the theory/catalog;
- the exact integer oracle mechanism was not exposed as a normal algorithm method;
- metadata/contracts were split inconsistently across headers and method registries changed shape at preprocessing time;
- README/docs were still centered on empirical campaigns rather than the permanent subject-repository role.

## Core library separation

`sqrtlab::sqrtlab` now contains only permanent subject code:

- scalar binary64 algorithms;
- scalar binary32 algorithms;
- seed mathematics/constants;
- exact binary32/binary64 arithmetic;
- hard-case construction;
- directed-rounding support;
- CPU batch/SIMD kernels.

It no longer links `src/experiment.cpp` or `src/float_experiment.cpp`.

Legacy benchmark support remains temporarily buildable behind:

```cmake
-DSQRTLAB_BUILD_LEGACY_BENCHMARKS=ON
```

and is excluded by default. The legacy experiment/statistics headers are not installed.

## Stable public API

Binary64 now has an explicit stable `F64Method` alias/registry (while retaining historical `Method` compatibility) with 16 methods:

1. `stdlib`
2. `hardware`
3. `newton`
4. `exponent-newton`
5. `bit-seed-newton`
6. `lut-newton`
7. `remez-newton`
8. `halley`
9. `goldschmidt`
10. `fma-goldschmidt`
11. `bisection`
12. `restoring`
13. `rsqrt-newton`
14. `fma-rsqrt-newton`
15. `hardware-rsqrt-newton`
16. `exact-integer`

Native binary32 now has 11 representative methods:

1. `stdlib-f32`
2. `hardware-f32`
3. `newton-f32`
4. `bit-seed-newton-f32`
5. `remez-newton-f32`
6. `halley-f32`
7. `goldschmidt-f32`
8. `bisection-f32`
9. `rsqrt-newton-f32`
10. `hardware-rsqrt-newton-f32`
11. `exact-integer-f32`

The f32 catalog intentionally does not clone every f64 seed/FMA parameterization.

Each registry is architecture-stable and carries:

- family;
- convergence;
- fixed refinement/step count;
- division flag;
- architecture/layout requirement;
- accuracy contract;
- description.

New capability-safe APIs:

- `try_compute_f64()` / `try_compute_f32()` return `std::nullopt` for unsupported methods;
- `compute_f64()` / `compute_f32()` return quiet NaN for unsupported method identifiers;
- architecture-specific methods never silently substitute another algorithm;
- registry metadata lookup functions are stable on all architectures.

Batch/SIMD metadata/capability queries follow the same rule.

## Algorithms/mechanisms added

### Binary64 Remez promoted to permanent catalog

The existing reproducible degree-4 minimax/Remez seed plus three Newton refinements is now a stable `remez-newton` binary64 method instead of research-only seed machinery.

### Exact integer algorithm exposed

The proved integer digit-recurrence oracle is now also exposed as:

- `F64Method::exact_integer`;
- `F32Method::exact_integer`.

These methods carry a proved `correctly_rounded`, 0-ULP contract.

### Native binary32 mechanism completion

Added materially distinct missing native-float mechanisms:

- Halley/higher-order rational iteration;
- Goldschmidt coupled refinement;
- portable reciprocal-square-root Newton reconstruction;
- exact integer digit recurrence.

These complete the representative f32 mechanism catalog without inflating it with redundant variants.

## Deliberately deferred from v1

Documented non-blocking deferrals:

- extra Householder/Bakhshali orders — Halley already represents the higher-order rational family;
- non-restoring/SRT variants — restoring/exact integer methods already represent digit recurrence for the general CPU catalog;
- CORDIC — distinct but primarily specialized-hardware/resource-constrained value, not required for general CPU v1;
- additional large direct-polynomial degree variants;
- binary128/extended precision;
- GPU kernels;
- every future SIMD ISA.

## Correctness/oracles

Preserved and separated as permanent subject functionality:

- exact binary64 integer oracle;
- exact binary32 integer oracle, now in its own translation unit;
- nearest/downward/upward/toward-zero exact rounding;
- binary64 exact local correction;
- deterministic adjacent-root rounding-boundary generator;
- 2,046-case minimum-margin normal binary64 corpus;
- optional MPFR differential cross-check support.

Special-value contract is now explicit: signed zero preserved, +infinity preserved, negative finite/-infinity -> NaN, NaN -> NaN, positive subnormals supported.

Accuracy contracts now live directly with stable method registries. Only exact-integer methods are marked proved correctly rounded; other ULP bounds remain deterministic regression guards.

## Deterministic core tests

The default core test graph is benchmark-independent and covers:

- registry/API/capability consistency;
- unsupported-method no-fallback behavior;
- special values and signed zero;
- subnormals, minimum normals, maximum finite values;
- exponent/power-of-two boundaries;
- deterministic random positive finite f32/f64 samples against exact oracles;
- declared accuracy contracts;
- exact correction/directed rounding through retained exact tests;
- deterministic rounding-boundary generation;
- all 2,046 minimum-margin exponent cases;
- seed validity/Remez envelope;
- batch metadata/support and exceptional-value behavior.

## Packaging

The project is now versioned as `1.0.0` and exports a real CMake package:

```cmake
add_subdirectory(path/to/sqrt)
target_link_libraries(app PRIVATE sqrtlab::sqrtlab)
```

or after installation:

```cmake
find_package(sqrtlab 1 CONFIG REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(app PRIVATE sqrtlab::sqrtlab)
```

Added:

- `sqrtlab::sqrtlab` alias target;
- GNU install directories;
- public-header FILE_SET;
- `sqrtlabTargets.cmake` export;
- relocatable `sqrtlabConfig.cmake`;
- version package file;
- installed license;
- umbrella `sqrtlab/sqrtlab.hpp`;
- public version header;
- basic consumer example.

Tests/tools/examples default OFF when sqrtlab is consumed through `add_subdirectory`.

## Documentation

README is rewritten around the permanent role: algorithms, API, correctness, theory, packaging, and explicit scope.

Added/updated:

- `docs/v1-scope.md` — definition of completeness and non-goals;
- `docs/api.md` — stable APIs and capability semantics;
- `docs/theory.md` — permanent algorithm taxonomy;
- `docs/native-binary32.md` — final f32 catalog/correctness role;
- `docs/benchmark-migration.md` — extraction boundary for `EddyTodd/bench`;
- `docs/roadmap.md` — remaining work is post-v1 or empirical;
- `results/README.md` — legacy evidence is explicitly pending migration.

## Validation

Performed during implementation before publication:

- GCC 14 C++23 strict-warning core build with warnings-as-errors: PASS;
- deterministic core test suites: PASS;
- strict-fenv build/test configuration: PASS;
- installed-package `find_package(sqrtlab)` consumer compile/run: PASS;
- `add_subdirectory` consumer compile/run: PASS;
- legacy benchmark support build boundary checked separately from the core.

After the agent interruption, the one source file whose tree attachment had been incomplete (`src/exact_f32.cpp`) was independently rechecked:

- GCC 14 `-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wconversion -Wshadow -Werror`: PASS;
- Clang 17 same strict warning class: PASS;
- 1,000,000 deterministic positive-finite binary32 inputs: nearest exact integer result matched platform `sqrtf` bit-for-bit;
- directed-rounding invariants (`downward == toward-zero`, nearest bracketed by downward/upward): PASS.

Hosted GitHub Actions were not used.

## Remaining core blockers

None identified for the declared v1 CPU binary32/binary64 scope.

Remaining work is either:

- empirical benchmark/research execution and infrastructure that belongs in `EddyTodd/bench`, or
- explicitly deferred post-v1 mechanisms/architectures described in `docs/v1-scope.md`.

## Future migration to `bench`

The main migration candidates are:

- `src/experiment.cpp`, `src/float_experiment.cpp`;
- `include/sqrtlab/experiment.hpp`, `include/sqrtlab/float_experiment.hpp`;
- timing/campaign/performance apps (`benchmark*`, `campaign`, `throughput`, `vector_study`, correction/seed performance studies, rounding campaign frontends where generic orchestration is involved);
- generic aggregation/campaign/provenance/report scripts;
- generic result schemas/manifests;
- retained benchmark result bundles under `results/`;
- generic evidence/campaign documentation.

Square-root-specific exact oracles, correction, hard-case generators, exhaustive correctness tools, method accuracy contracts, seed mathematics, and theory remain in `sqrt`.
